[{:seon.task/name "a-process-says-what-it-is"
  :seon.task/plan "In Activity Monitor and ps, every process a root forks (the fork server, its standby, each agent, clone and test child) shows the root's own command line, ./seon root PORT, so the owner can't tell a root from an agent or which checkout it belongs to (14 processes across five checkouts, all named alike). Owner: runtime (process lifecycle). What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: nothing renames a process: no setproctitle or argv write in src (git grep proctitle/argv: only seon.config/argv, the reader of root's args, config.clj:213); every fork goes through seon.lib/fork! (lib.clj:257) or jolt.async.remote.flow/fork! (test children, agent.clj:1347). The cause: a fork copies the parent's argv area and nothing in the child writes its own. The design: one write in the one spawning skeleton, never per caller: seon.lib/fork! takes the child's title, its role and identity (\"seon agent a3 (next-wiki)\", \"seon fork-server (next-wiki)\", \"seon test a3-child-2\"), and the child writes it over its argv area as its first act (macOS: what setproctitle does, the argv strings rewritten in place, so ps -o command= shows it; the bytes are bounded by the original argv's length, a longer title truncated). The title is derived from the process's own facts (role, :seon.agent/key, the checkout's name), never a hand list, and the same string is a fact on its entity (:seon.process/title, schema entry with it). Test children route through lib/fork! (runtime's hot-path note: the test child's second exit discipline goes), so they get it with no second site. Deletes: nothing; adds one argument to fork!. Estimate: one memcpy of under 256 bytes per fork, ~10k ticks; the test spawns one agent (~3M). Proofs, red first: seon.agent-test/a-spawned-agent-names-its-role-and-checkout-in-ps (after spawning an agent and its test child, ps -o command= for each pid names its role; red today: both read ./seon root PORT); design guard seon.main-test/every-fork-goes-through-lib-fork (a law over src: no call of jolt.host/fork-alone or c-fork outside seon.lib, so a process started another way cannot skip its title)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-spawned-agent-names-its-role-and-checkout-in-ps seon.main-test/every-fork-goes-through-lib-fork}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-death-names-its-cause"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: two runs ended `:closed` with reason \"agent a9 died\" and nothing more, while root's log held the causes: an uncaught `{:type :fatal-commit-error}` from Datahike in `seon.repl/record!`, and a ClassCastException (nil cannot be cast to IFn) in `seon.agent/run-tests-forked`, plausibly after the agent redefined a platform function its own loop calls (a real REPL may; the merge judges). Owner: runtime (the agent process's lifecycle). What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: root's seon.system/end-agent makes a death the error :seon.agent/died with the exit status, the stderr tail (seon.agent/stderr-path, :seon.config.root/death-stderr-bytes) and its last op (system.clj:405-425); a throw in a request's step is answered with its error (seon.repl/defect-answer, repl.clj:1429; seon.agent-test/a-step-that-throws-answers-its-error-and-the-agent-goes-on); a forked child that throws out of its code prints to stderr and exits 1 (seon.lib/fork!, lib.clj:277-288); root's own process sets the default uncaught-exception handler (seon.system/catch-uncaught!, system.clj:1783-1790), but nothing in seon.agent, seon.repl or seon.fork-server sets one, so an agent's uncaught error goes where its inherited handler sends it [H: to root's uncaught! copied by the fork, whose :back is root's channel, not reachable from the child, so it is kept under :early and lost at exit]. The cause: the agent process has no one edge that turns an exception ending it into data; what survives is whatever the stderr tail happens to hold. The design: the agent's entrant boundary is one named edge (docs/failure-points.md): its init! sets the process's default uncaught handler and the fork child's catch in lib/fork! hands its throwable to the same function, which writes the one error map (:seon.error/type, :seon.error/operation, message, cause chain, bounded by seon.contract/bounded) as the run's end on its branch and commits, before the process exits; root's judge carries it into the run's reason and evidence (:seon.agent/died gains :seon.agent/end from the branch, the stderr tail kept beside it). Then find the fatal commit's cause from that evidence (a task of its own, data's). Deletes: the reliance on the stderr tail as the only cause (kept as evidence). Estimate: nothing per eval; at a death one write and one commit (~1M ticks). Proofs, red first: seon.agent-test/an-agent-that-dies-outside-a-request-names-its-error (an eval starts a thread that throws an ex-info and then calls c-exit: the run's reason names the ex-info's type, red today: \"agent .. died\" and a stderr tail); seon.agent-test/a-step-that-throws-answers-its-error-and-the-agent-goes-on (green, kept: the in-request half)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-step-that-throws-answers-its-error-and-the-agent-goes-on seon.agent-test/an-agent-that-dies-outside-a-request-names-its-error}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-reports-its-threads"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed by worktree session agent-view (2026-10-23): the owner asked the agent's page to show its process's threads and uptime. Owner: runtime (the agent process). What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: the agent's :stats answer carries mem-mb, t, pid, results, evals and heap-mb, no thread count (seon.agent/handle! :stats, agent.clj:2016-2018), nor does its hello (seon.agent/hello!, agent.clj:2033-2037); seon.lib/active-threads (lib.clj:244) reads the active Chez thread count, the one count fork! already reads; the page shows :seon.agent/started-ms. The cause: no one asked the measure. The design: the agent's :stats and hello carry :threads (seon.lib/active-threads, a measured fact, as mem-mb is), the agent proc keeps it in root's view entry beside mem-mb (no new proc, no poll: it rides the frames the agent already sends), and the page's head shows it; the head shows the run's start (started-ms), never a ticking uptime (a clock deciding a render: docs/clocks.md#if-nothing-has-changed-we-do-nothing). Deletes nothing. Estimate: one FFI call per :stats (~1k ticks). Proof, red first: seon.agent-test/an-agent-reports-its-threads (an agent's :stats answer and root's view of it hold :threads ≥ 1; after an eval starts an io-thread that parks, one more; red today: no key)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-reports-its-threads}}
 {:seon.task/name "fork-error-names-the-threads"
  :seon.task/plan "Superseded in its first half [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: seon.lib/fork! no longer refuses beside a thread; it waits for the others to park (jolt.host/fork-alone, bounded by :seon.config.agent/fork-wait-ms and cancelled only by jolt.host/fork-alone-cancel!, lib.clj:257-290), proved by seon.lib-test/a-fork-beside-a-running-thread-waits-for-it-to-park and seon.lib-test/a-fork-whose-wait-is-cancelled-says-how-many-run; the fork's own bound naming its holders is deps' (seon.lib/a-forks-wait-ends-at-its-declared-bound-naming-its-holders). Owner of what is left: runtime (forking). What is left [M]: seon.agent's interrupt-self-test spins up to 100,000 evals of (eval 1) waiting for its own SIGUSR1 (agent.clj:726-735), run at every agent's start (init!, agent.clj:2166). The cause: a wait for a signal by counting work (a spin bounded by a hand constant) instead of the one safe point where Chez runs a pending handler. The design: the self-test raises SIGUSR1 to itself, then passes exactly one safe point (one engine tick: Chez delivers pending signal handlers at its next event check, jolt.host's own) and reads whether the handler ran; nothing loops. If one tick is shown not to be enough [H, settled by the proof], the wait is a fiber parked on a channel the handler puts to, bounded by a put-after! due, never a count. Deletes: the 100,000 loop and its eval per iteration. Estimate: one signal and one safe point, ~10k ticks per agent start, against up to 100,000 evals today (not measured: measure the spin at one start before the change, seon.profile/measure! around interrupt-self-test). Proof, red first: seon.agent-test/the-interrupt-self-test-needs-one-safe-point (the self-test's ticks are under 100k and it answers true in a process forked from a main thread; red under the mutation that restores the loop: its ticks scale with the count)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/the-interrupt-self-test-needs-one-safe-point}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-green-under-the-runner-is-green-in-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by a worked example (evaluations session, 2026-10-23): seon.repl-test/an-error-in-an-anonymous-fn-is-in-the-fn-around-it and seon.repl-test/an-eval-error-says-where-in-its-forms-own-text were green under bin/test on main (cb77a911) but red with clojure.test/test-vars at any agent's REPL (6 assertions: :seon.error/at and :seon.error/in differ, e.g. {:line 1} for {:line 3}, rewrite-clj.zip.seqz/map-seq for nil); the judge's fork ran the first red too, and the done rule held the example's run for it as a stray red. A test whose verdict depends on where it runs judges nothing. Owner: runtime (program loading: an agent loads its program from the branch's recorded forms, the runner from files). What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: both deftests are still in test/seon/repl_test.clj (:1062, :1082); an agent's program comes from seon.program/load-diff! over the fork server's (agent.clj:2200) and a runner's from files; whether they still differ at HEAD was not run [H]. The cause [H]: the position Jolt reports for a form loaded from its :form/src string (line 1 of that string) differs from the one read from its file (the file's line), and the frames named differ because a loaded form's fn names differ; the one start (seon.fx/runtime-for then seon.program/load-diff!, plan item 3a) makes every process load the same way, which may close it. The design: settle first by running the two tests both ways at HEAD (one eval at an agent's REPL, one bin/test of the namespace); if still red, the producer of :seon.error/at (seon.repl's position of a form within its source) reads the form's recorded :form/line offset so a form loaded from the store reports its file's line, and the fn name comes from the same var naming in both; never a special case in the test. Deletes: nothing [H]. Estimate: the reproduction is two runs of two tests (~10M ticks); the fix is O(1) per error. Proofs, red first: seon.agent-test/a-repl-tests-verdict-in-an-agent-is-the-runners (the two repl tests run by test-vars in an agent: the same verdict and the same :seon.error/at as under the runner); design guard: the one start (seon.program/a-process-runs-its-branchs-program) is the only loader, so no second path loads forms another way."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-repl-tests-verdict-in-an-agent-is-the-runners}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-first-read-costs-what-a-later-one-does"
  :seon.task/plan "A fresh agent's first request build (seon.turn/context-block) cost about 126M ticks on a worktree root of 908 tasks, against 22-33M for a later one: its first observe 50M against about 8M warm, its first render about 40M more than a later one. Owner: runtime (forking: what a fork inherits). Done [M]: seon.db/after-fork! keeps each node cache of the parent's connections alone and the agent's first connect adopts it (db.clj:1052-1063, datahike.connections/after-fork!); measured on fresh agents, the first observe 21.6M (3 misses), the first render no dearer than a later one (33.8M against 32.6M), a larger task's whole first read 79M against 61.5M later; proved by seon.db-test/a-fork-keeps-its-parents-node-caches-and-no-connection. What is left [M, 2026-11]: about 14M of the first observe is first-time work that is no store read: seon.problems/caused-split reads sections, var names and catch forms the fork server never decoded (20.4M against 4.6M, with base and head equal). The cause: derived values the fork server could hold warm are computed after the fork, per agent, once each (AGENTS §3a.3: done once, kept with its source). The design: what caused-split derives from main's program (its sections, var names, catch forms) is derived once where main's program is (the fork server, at each commit it applies) and inherited by every fork, never recomputed per agent; under one derivation (plan item 5) it becomes a reaction over the store the fork carries. Deletes: the per-agent first derivation. Estimate: a first read within 1.2x + 2M of a later one, at any size of main (the per-fork work O(0) in the program). Proofs, red first: seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-read-costs-what-a-later-one-does (a fresh agent's first observe and a second one, measured in ticks on a root of 100 tasks and of 400: first ≤ 1.2x second + 2M at both; red today by ~14M); seon.db-test/a-fork-keeps-its-parents-node-caches-and-no-connection (green, kept)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-read-costs-what-a-later-one-does seon.db-test/a-fork-keeps-its-parents-node-caches-and-no-connection}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-spawns-start-costs-the-same-on-a-bigger-program"
  :seon.task/plan "Red on main before the 2026-10-28 batch and taken out of seon.agent-test then: an agent spawned after main gained 200 forms had a :state phase of 616,608 bytes against 460,304 before, over the bound 1.1x + 64 KiB. A spawn's own work must not grow with main's program (the owner, 2026-10-25). Owner: runtime (spawning). What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: the spawn's phases are marked by the fork server and the agent (seon.agent/spawn-phases, agent.clj:2021-2029; fork_server.clj:226) and sent with the hello; the deftest was removed by 91ac5dfe34 (its text at 139bbb75ff, test/seon/agent_test.clj); seon.fork-server-cost-test holds graph-at-fork flat in the program. Whether the :state phase still grows was not run [H]. The cause [H]: something read or built per form in init!'s state phase (the ctx record's pull, the reach graph or index handed at fork, the branch views). The design: restore the deftest at 139bbb75ff's text, run it once; if red, find the per-form read by seon.profile/measure! on the :state phase at two sizes, and make it O(the branch's change): what main's program derives is held by the fork server and inherited (as an-agents-first-read-costs-what-a-later-one-does), never rebuilt in the child; if green, the restored test is the proof and the task is done. Deletes: nothing [H]. Estimate: the :state phase constant in main's size, within 1.1x + 64 KiB from 0 to 200 more forms. Proof, red first (or green at once, which closes the task): seon.agent-test/a-spawns-start-costs-the-same-on-a-bigger-program."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-spawns-start-costs-the-same-on-a-bigger-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-budget-comes-from-measured-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: a budget where 90%+ of the time it's under budget and the rare overrun is mostly a time we should intervene (docs/decisions.md). Superseded in its method by later rulings: a budget is worked from what the work must do, never the last run (AGENTS §3 step 2; seon.fx/typical-ticks' docstring, fx.clj:1035-1038, \"never its budget\"), and every bound is a :seon.bound/bound on the request (owner review in seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer). So the plan to derive an eval's budget from a population of past evals goes. Owner: runtime (effects and bounds). What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: an eval's ticks bound is the request's :ticks, else :seon.config.agent/form-ticks (20e9, default.edn:310) for the origins seon.repl/budgeted names; its bytes bound :seon.config.agent/form-bytes is 0, none (default.edn:320) (seon.repl/eval-string, repl.clj:173-180); an eval's wall time has no bound of its own (the stall check stands in: seon.agent-proc/a-stalled-eval-ends-with-its-error). The cause: an eval's bounds are three separate knobs read at one site, not the request's one :seon.bound/bound. The design: an eval request carries :seon.bound/bound (ticks, bytes, ms); its effective bound is the least per key of the eval request schema's malli :default (ticks 20e9 with its reason, ms the stall default), the request's own and a config override through the one resolver; seon.repl reads that one map; an overrun is the typed :seon.bound/reached with :seon.bound/spent (replacing :seon.repl/ticks-spent and bytes-spent) and is recorded on the eval as a fact root can show (an intervention point, the owner's 90% rule measured on it, never deciding it). Deletes: :seon.config.agent/form-ticks and form-bytes as separate keys (folded into the schema default), seon.repl/budgeted's origin list as the decider (an origin's default is the request schema's). Estimate: no change per eval (one map read). Proofs, red first: seon.agent-test/an-eval-past-its-bound-is-answered-bound-reached-and-recorded (an eval with {:seon.bound/ticks 1000} spinning: answered :seon.bound/reached, its spent on the eval record); design guard seon.main-test/a-bound-is-a-config-key (exists: every bound a config key) extended so no src reads :seon.config.agent/form-ticks outside the request schema's default."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-eval-past-its-bound-is-answered-bound-reached-and-recorded seon.main-test/a-bound-is-a-config-key}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-reader-of-an-agents-branch-sees-what-its-answered-evals-wrote"
  :seon.task/plan "seon.repl-test/an-edit-in-an-agent-is-program-on-its-branch went red once in batch 66's merge prove (2026-10-31, ff5f74f128; a 44-test shard) and green alone twice at the same tree: the agent answered its edit! (:edited), then root's eval read the agent's branch (seon.system/branch-db) and the edited var's :var/form held the old source. So an eval's answer can reach its asker before the commit holding what it wrote is readable by another process. Owner: runtime (the agent process's serve loop). What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: seon.agent/commit! commits pending writes in memory (dw/commit-pending!) and sends {:op :committed :t} to root, whose flusher makes it durable (agent.clj:327-349); the eval paths commit before answering (repl.clj:1193, :1206, :1233), the tests proc commits only when not :busy (repl.clj:1383); seon.system/branch-db opens the branch from the store, which another process sees only once flushed [H]. The cause [H]: the answer is sent when the commit is in this process's memory; a reader in another process reads the store, which holds it only after root's flush, so an answer is not a promise that a reader sees it (docs/storage.md#durability-is-continuous-group-commit: an outside effect waits for the sync that covers it). The design: the answer carries the commit it follows ({:seon.db/branch :seon.db/commit :seon.db/t}, the kept-db reference), and a reader acting on an answer reads at that reference (seon.system/branch-db with the commit), which root's writer answers once its flush covers it, a wait ended by the flush's message, never a clock; no reader guesses. Deletes: branch-db's read of the branch head after an answer. Estimate: one more key per answer; a read at a commit costs what a head read does. Proofs, red first: seon.agent-test/an-answered-evals-write-is-on-the-branch-its-answer-names (an eval's answer names a commit, and root's branch-db at that commit holds the write, with the flusher held back by a message until the read asks: the read waits for it, never answers the old value; red today: the answer names no commit); seon.repl-test/an-edit-in-an-agent-is-program-on-its-branch (green, kept)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-answered-evals-write-is-on-the-branch-its-answer-names seon.repl-test/an-edit-in-an-agent-is-program-on-its-branch}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-agents-state-is-declared"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\". Owner: runtime (the agent's state; holdout H2 of every-stateful-process-is-a-flow-agent-in-one-tree-with-ports-named-by-message-keys). What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: seon.agent/state is one process-global atom (agent.clj:36), its keys described in a comment (agent.clj:24), with 52 swap!/reset! sites in agent.clj, 21 in repl.clj and 6 in turn.clj and no validator; its schema is :seon.agent/state [:map] with only a render (resources/seon/schemas/seon.render.edn:10). The agent's three procs (transport, repl, tests) share it instead of owning it, so Flow's revert-on-throw does not cover it. Five fixes were this class: d1ab45ec12 and 24b5508112 (socket and intake reader lost when state was replaced), c4a4cdd495 and 2c3867e897 (a test child inherited the interrupt and the page walk, reset by hand at one fork site), 42854c8c33 (a carry read :db from state). The cause: a mutable map shared by three procs, with no declared keys and no declared fate across a fork. The design (the Flow way, owner 2026-11-14: \"once the flow system is up and running ... we must only use flow based systems for stateful processes\"): the state becomes each proc's own, in slices, each deleting its keys from the atom: (1) the transport's keys (:waits, :fx-pending, :tx-seq) the transport proc's state; (2) :busy and :draining messages to the repl proc; (3) what an eval reads (:db :branch :key :program) one read-only value the repl proc hands each eval; (4) what is left a closed malli map :seon.agent/state, each key with :seon.agent/on-fork :reset or :keep, the test child's state derived from it in place of the hand dissoc and resets; host handles stay out (in link). seon.intr's stack and pending atoms are checked the same way. Docs: docs/agent.md, docs/flow.md. Deletes: the atom's keys slice by slice; the hand resets at the fork site. Estimate: no cost per eval (a proc's state is a value it already holds); slice (1) about -60 +40 lines. Proofs, red first: seon.agent-test/a-forked-childs-state-follows-its-schema (a test child's state holds exactly the :keep keys of its parent's); seon.agent-test/a-step-that-throws-leaves-the-transports-waits-as-they-were (Flow's revert covers the transport's keys: red today, a throw after a swap! keeps it); design guard seon.main-test/no-atom-outside-a-proc-holds-a-procs-state (a law over src: no new swap! of seon.agent/state; the count only falls)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-forked-childs-state-follows-its-schema seon.agent-test/a-step-that-throws-leaves-the-transports-waits-as-they-were seon.main-test/no-atom-outside-a-proc-holds-a-procs-state}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refused-exit-watch-is-an-answer"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-04 in bin/check scale at 100 agents (76ab975c96): one test child failed with `jolt.io-poller/exit-chan: kevent refused the watch`, ex-data {:pid 4300 :errno 35} (EAGAIN), thrown under seon.agent/read-to-exit, then `Exception in go/fiber body (channel closed)`: 99 of 100 ok. Owner: runtime (test children; Jolt's half deps'). What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: Jolt's exit-watch now asks EAGAIN again (ask-while-eagain) and still throws on another errno or a persisting EAGAIN (reference-code/jolt stdlib/jolt/io_poller.clj:1017-1045); seon.agent/fork-test-child watches each child's exit on an a/io-thread blocking on (poller/exit-chan os) and puts {:seon.process/pid :seon.process/exit} on the run's ends (agent.clj:1352): a throw from exit-chan there has no catcher, so the fold never gets that child's end and waits on its link alone. The cause: an outside-world failure point (a kernel watch) throws on a thread instead of answering (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs), and the exit watch is a thread where a fiber does (holdout H3). The design: the watch is a go block (a fiber; exit-chan is a channel already), and a refused watch answers the waiter as data: the child's :seon.process/end carries :seon.error/type :seon.process/watch-refused {:pid :errno}, and the fold then reaps by waitpid when the link ends (the child's death is still known); named in docs/failure-points.md with its edge function. Deletes: the io-thread per test child. Estimate: no thread per child (one fiber, ~1 KB); no change per test. Proofs, red first: seon.agent-test/a-refused-exit-watch-answers-its-waiter (exit-chan made to throw for one child, with-redefs at the agent's REPL: the run's answer names the child's watch-refused end and its tests' verdicts; red today: the run never gets that end); seon.agent-test/an-agent-running-three-test-children-holds-no-thread-per-child (design guard for H3: lib/active-threads unchanged by three children)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-refused-exit-watch-answers-its-waiter seon.agent-test/an-agent-running-three-test-children-holds-no-thread-per-child}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-reply-is-a-message-with-a-to"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: its plan keeps :seon.message/call, which Context's message-delivery re-model removes; Context rewrites it against that re-model first. Owner, 2026-11-07: reply text becomes a message, and an agent won't always be talking to a person. Today `message-md` is stored as `:seon.model-call/reply` and reaches nobody; a person sees it by watching the page, and `seon.reconcile/tell-tx` tells who started a task only at its end. Correct: the eval tool takes an optional `to`, naming agents by key; `message-md` becomes a message from this agent to them, with no `to` staying on its branch. `chat-message` and `reply-hiccup` become one render: from me is role `assistant`, else `user`, and a reply keeps ``:seon.message/call`` so a provider's pairing with its evals holds. `docs/prompt.md`'s 'your user' changes. Context's first: the request's prefix bytes stay stable across turns. Proof: a chat agent's reply reaches a person's `:messages`; the wire is byte-identical for a transcript with no `to`. See docs/users.md#messages."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-words-are-clojures"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-08, yes to a word sweep: names and prose use Clojure's, its libraries' or git's words, or say exactly what happens (docs/vocabulary.md#words-we-do-not-use; docs/decisions.md). Found: take and untaken (seon.agent, seon.system, seon.repl), where git's or Datahike's word for what happens fits; session, which means an agent's env up with a process, on a branch in the db, taking turns (owner, 2026-11-08), so a text that means something else says what it means; {:branch :db} maps, where a point is {:branch :commit :t} (docs/decisions.md, never pass databases); selection, which says nothing (bin/prove deciding which deftests to run); kinds, which become maps chosen by merge (no kind enums). One rename a commit, each with every reader; a stored keyword is renamed by erasing the store, so those wait for the next erase (the content-hashed store, docs/sharing.md#what-a-branchs-nodes-are). Proof: the old names are gone from src, test and docs (git grep), and bin/prove is green."
