 {:seon.task/name "a-real-gate-is-proved-in-seconds"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-10: \"If that test isn't god's gift to the world fucking kill it\": ``seon.agent-test/a-real-gate-judges-a-candidate-once`` (150-174 s median, the largest test) is deleted, and its claim (a prove on a checkout of the candidate, its verdict reaching the branch, the judge reusing it) has no test until this lands, at seconds. Its history: it was the only test of a real gate (a prove on a checkout of the candidate, its verdict reaching the branch, the judge reusing it): seon.fx-test/a-gates-red-runs-again-and-its-green-does-not and seon.agent-test/a-process-test-gets-its-verdict-while-the-agent-can-act stub the gate, so deleting it (a test agent did, reverted) loses the claim. It took about 95 s (median, 2026-11-08; the 247 s was older). Landed ce2c353182: it runs on the shard's root, each shared root on a merges branch of its own (seon.test-store/start-shared-root!), about 8 s fewer in a shard with other agent tests; its digest names the gate code it reaches across root's :gate message (a stopgap for seon.proofs/a-process-tests-reads-hold-what-its-agent-ran). Measured 97 s alone after. What is left is the gate's own work on every agent's candidate, measured by hand: the worktree 3 s, bin/build seon 26 s (21 s Jolt's link: the build key covers the whole tree, test/ included, so a candidate that only adds a test rebuilds), the nested plan 16 s (a fresh worktree's program store starts cold), the nested seed 25 s (5,128 forms into a new process store), the shard 7 s. Two ways, each a design change held for the owner: (a) a build key over only what jolt build reads (seon.tree is also what bin/check compares); (b) the gate's program and process stores kept between gate runs beside its main-build slot (a re-seed of a clone measured 3 s, not 25 s; about 35 s off every production gate, not this test's, which starts empty). What stands since (2026-11-10, jolt/tests-cut): the agent's side of a gate that cannot run is seon.agent-test/a-gate-that-cannot-run-is-the-proves-answer (its typed error is the prove's answer; a run that reached no test is a verdict that is not green), and the no-plan refusal held per candidate is seon.fx-test/a-candidate-that-makes-no-plan-is-not-built-again. Its red of 2026-11-10 was a product defect (a gate's checkout ran its root's binary on the main tree's runtime, fixed: docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches), and it read as {:proofs nil} only because its select-keys dropped the typed error my.agent/prove answered: a real-gate test asserts on the whole answer. What has no test: a real checkout's prove reaching the branch. Proof, once (a) or (b) makes it seconds: a real gate on a one-test candidate, its verdict on the branch and the judge's second ask running nothing, its :ms under 30 s."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-user-agent-lives-on-its-own-branch"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-09: \"I want the user to have a process regardless. Anything that's user focused should be handled by this agent too so separate users don't take down the system\"; \"User should have a branch just like the other agents\"; \"The user isn't modifying anything it's sending the requests to agents\". Stage 4 of docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09: runtime the lifecycle and the branch, web the user agent's walk and root's transport of it. What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: the user is an agent entity with no process (:seon.agent/key plus :seon.user/name, seon.task/boot-tx, task.clj:456-462); the reconciler's actions read only tasks (seon.reconcile/actions, reconcile.clj:507); init! tolerates no task (agent.clj:2236 begins a run only with one); opening an eval writes :seon.agent/opened on main (seon.system/opened!, system.clj:2348; the api at :2009) and the agent folds it from main (seon.agent/untaken-facts, agent.clj:188-200, called at repl.clj:1325); nothing writes :seon.agent/kept. The cause: a user's state and requests are written on main by root, so a user's load is root's and main is not root's alone. The design (approved by web for the owner, 2026-11-09): desired state as data, :seon.agent/kept (optional boolean, schema entry) written by boot for each user; one reconciler rule: an agent with :seon.agent/kept and no live process is spawned on its own branch (seon.system/spawn!, no task), its death recorded as any agent's and respawned at the next pass, never a watchdog; it counts in max-running and never enters :ready, by its data. Its branch is cut at its first spawn and resumed after (init!'s resumed path, load-diff!). It has no turn loop and parks in Flow when idle; its UI state is on its branch. Opening an eval is an :eval with origin :context it sends the agent (a busy agent's REPL takes :context evals at its parks); seon.system/opened!, :seon.agent/opened and seon.agent/untaken-facts go. The renders it asks its agents for are derived, evictable data in its process, never in @ctx. Deletes: opened!, :seon.agent/opened, untaken-facts and its call in repl.clj. Estimate: about +40 -25 lines; one idle process per user (its heap, measured at its first spawn); no cost to root per request. Proofs, red first: seon.agent-test/a-user-agent-is-running-after-boot-and-again-after-it-is-killed; seon.agent-test/a-user-agents-ui-state-is-on-its-branch-and-none-on-main; seon.agent-test/an-open-reaches-the-agents-branch-and-its-next-render; design guard seon.main-test/root-writes-no-users-state-on-main (no write of :seon.user/* other than boot's on main)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-user-agent-is-running-after-boot-and-again-after-it-is-killed seon.agent-test/a-user-agents-ui-state-is-on-its-branch-and-none-on-main seon.agent-test/an-open-reaches-the-agents-branch-and-its-next-render seon.main-test/root-writes-no-users-state-on-main}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-runs-measures-are-their-own-entities"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-09: re-model 1, yes: each run's measures are their own entity with a ref to what they measured, summed when read. Step 1 (a function's cost) is built on jolt/sync: ``:seon.run/id`` (unique per write), ``:seon.run/of``, ``:seon.run/agent``, ``:seon.run/stats``, summed by ``seon.agent/fn-cost``; :seon.fn/stats gone. Step 2, not built, lands with the Context session: a test's results as runs (``:seon.run/digest``, status, failures, ticks, bytes); a test's verdict is its newest run at its current digest, flaky being two statuses at one digest. Writers: seon.proofs' test-facts, facts-worth-keeping, result-tx and seon.agent/record-results!. Readers to move: seon.agent's run-tests-forked, with-gate-verdicts and judge!, the task pattern's :proved pull, ctx/status (Context's) and render/ui's proofs-green count (Web's). Deletes the five :seon.test/* attributes on the var and seon.proofs' flaky/sum merge logic. About 80-120 lines; a store erase."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/test-s-verdict-newest-seon-run-entity-current}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-done-counts-only-the-reds-its-change-reaches"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-10 (Context's baseline of three Sonnet agents on main f4968a8624, its agent-base worktree root; the run's path is in the managing session's notes folder): an agent's own proofs went green, and its task was closed :closed on two reds it never touched, seon.program-test/a-test-that-starts-a-process-reaches-the-program-it-runs and seon.render-test/a-render-reaches-no-process (over their 4B-tick budget inside a big prove, green alone: task seon.proofs/a-tests-ticks-are-the-same-alone-and-in-a-run). The class: a done rule that charges an agent with a red its change does not reach, so any stray red on main closes every task. What correct looks like (managing session, 2026-11-10): a red counts against a task only when the task names the test as a proof (seon.task/proof?) or the branch's change reaches it, its reach digest on the branch differing from base's (what the plan already stores: seon.agent/test-plan's digests against base's); a red at a digest equal to base's is main's red, told to its area as a task (seon.prove/a-red-in-the-daily-full-test-is-a-task) and never closes the agent's task. Read the judge path first (seon.agent's judge and done rule, seon.task/proof?, seon.reconcile's end of a run) and say in the landing what the rule replaces. Proof, red first: a world where base holds a red test the branch's change does not reach and the task's proof is green: the task merges; the same red reached by the change: it does not. Absorbed in curation 2026-11-14 (same subject and evidence): seon.agent/the-done-rule-holds-under-load (Seen 2026-11-02 in the optimization session's bin/prove --merge of jolt/one-search at load 15-19 (logs target/prove/20261102T110219-57930/shard-2.log): seon.agent-test/a-task-whose-proofs-pass-is-done-only-when-what-it-reaches-is-green answered [:green :merged []], the change that breaks main's edit test merged with no reason named; the same test alone at load ~8 is green (refused, the stray red n); seon.check/a-swarm-task-is-closed-on-a-regression-it-did-not-cause (Not ready: seen once and not reproduced, its cause only inferred; a reproduction (or a second sighting with the run's store kept) has to show a defect first)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/world-where-base-holds-red-test-branch-s}}
 {:seon.task/name "each-eval-is-its-own-commit-is-a-proof-that-can-fail"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 by runtime (then Optimization, fixing its flake on jolt/evalflake 9ccfcc76c4): seon.agent-test/an-agent-commits-after-each-eval stayed green when seon.turn/evaluated! dropped its per-eval commit, because the scripted turn made one model eval a turn. Done at HEAD [M, read 6184c92521]: the test now makes one model call of three evals (seon.fixtures/three-calls) and walks back from the branch head asserting no commit adds more than one eval record, a merge commit walked to its parent on this branch (test/seon/agent_test.clj:3173-3190), which is the correction this task asked for. Not yet shown: that it is red under the mutation (a dropped per-eval commit) [H]; that one run is the proof's last step. Owner: runtime. Proposed: once the mutation run is seen red, delete this entry. Proof: seon.agent-test/an-agent-commits-after-each-eval, red under the mutation that drops seon.turn/evaluated!'s commit."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-commits-after-each-eval}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-request-costs-the-same-however-many-came-before"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by runtime (2026-11-10, measuring main's content mark, docs/storage.md#mains-content-mark): one agent answering an outside eval of 1 through root's server (bin/ask), 15 requests a round, three rounds, load 12-18: with the mark the p50 went 100 -> 122 -> 122 ms; with the mark's skip off it went 152 -> 199 -> 220 ms. The second growth is understood (merge-main!'s base walk and content diffs span every commit since the base; the mark removes it). The first, ~20 ms over 45 requests with the mark, is not explained (a wall-time measure under load, so not yet evidence by AGENTS §5: re-measure in ticks). Owner: runtime. What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: per request the agent reads seon.agent/untaken-facts over main's :seon.agent/opened (agent.clj:188-200; repl.clj:1325) and commits (seon.agent/commit!), and the turn reads its own evals through the pull. Suspects [H]: something per request kept on the agent's branch and read whole each request (its evals, the page's walk over the conversation), or untaken-facts' read of main. The design: measure first, in ticks: seon.profile/measure! inside the agent around seon.turn/outside-reply! at requests 10, 100 and 300 on one agent, each phase; then the cause fixed at its owner so a request reads O(1) of the requests before it (a pull of the last eval, never the whole :seon.eval/_agent; untaken-facts goes with the-user-agent-lives-on-its-own-branch). Estimate: a request's ticks at 300 within 1.1x + 1M of at 10. Proof, red first: seon.agent-test/an-agents-request-costs-the-same-however-many-came-before (ticks of request 300 against request 10, on one agent, held to that bound)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agents-request-costs-the-same-however-many-came-before}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-fiber-error-reaches-its-reply"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (first draft by deps, 2026-11-11). What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: an eval's own throw comes back in its reply as :ex (seon.repl/wrap-caught). Jolt (c3742a8a, pinned) hands every uncaught error of a thread, go block, fiber body, fiber-execute task or transducer completion to the handler a program sets with Thread/setDefaultUncaughtExceptionHandler (docs/forks.md#jolt). Root sets one for its own process (seon.system/catch-uncaught!, system.clj:1783-1790, its errors a :platform problem on main); the agent process sets none (no setDefaultUncaughtExceptionHandler in seon.agent, seon.repl or seon.fork-server), so a go loop an eval started that dies after the eval returned is lost to the agent [H: it reaches root's handler copied by the fork, whose :back is not this process's]. The cause: the agent has no edge for its own uncaught errors. The design: the agent process's one entrant edge (the same as a-death-names-its-cause's) sets the handler at init!; an error it receives becomes a message from the system entity to this agent (the existing message mechanism, docs/users.md#messages: no new attribute), naming the eval whose form was running when the body was spawned when that is known (seon.repl binds the current eval's handle; Jolt's spawn captures bindings), else the agent itself, the error as data (docs/errors.md); the agent reads it at its next request. Not this task: an error inside the eval's own form (already :ex). Deletes: nothing. Estimate: nothing per eval; per uncaught error one message write. Proof, red first: seon.agent-test/an-evals-fiber-error-reaches-its-reply (an eval starts a go block that throws after the form returns; the agent's next request holds the error and names the eval; red today: nothing reaches it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-evals-fiber-error-reaches-its-reply}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-names-the-agent-it-continues"
  :seon.task/plan "Waits on the owner's choice (a session is an agent, or an entity of its own: the two shapes as forms are in the session notes, 2026-11-11); built the day he says the first. What exists, read: a fork makes a sibling task named with a -fork- suffix and a number, copies the plan and proofs, and starts an agent told at its start which agent it is a clone of (```seon.server/fork!```); that is an argument of the start and is stored nowhere, so afterwards only the task's name says where the fork came from. Who started an agent is stored (the starter's agents), and where its branch was cut (its base commit); whose conversation it goes on from is not. Gate, why the existing refs cannot hold it: the starter of a fork may be a person or the system, not the agent forked; a task's example sessions are sessions to show whoever works the task, not what this agent continues. Plan: one ref on the agent, to the agent whose conversation it continues, written where a start is given one (the fork; a go-back; a rerun that should read the run before it). The name is settled at a REPL first. Its readers, as forms on a pulled agent c: the agent it came from, (get c the-ref); a fork's whole conversation, the source's members up to the fork's base and then its own, by one join to a depth in the agent's pattern; a reflect fork's subject, the same ref (so a reflect the system starts needs nothing else); the forks of an agent, the ref reversed. A rerun finds the run before it by order among the task's agents and needs this ref only if it should read that run's conversation. Deletes: the -fork- and number in a task's name as the only trace, and the clone-of argument as a fact nobody can read later. Proof, red first: an agent forked from another holds the ref, its pull reaches the source's evals before the cut and none after, and the source's pull lists its forks."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-branch-reads-its-tasks-execution-from-main"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime's one-writer design (deps found it). A task's :seon.task/execution has one writer, root, on main; an agent's branch reads it from main as heard (seon.agent/heard-main), never a copy it writes. What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: seon.agent/begin-run-tx still writes {:seon.task/id task :seon.task/execution :running} on the agent's branch (agent.clj:2122-2132), called by begin-run! at init! (agent.clj:2103-2120, :2236) and by the fork server's warm-start; so :running has two writers. Tried as 053ed1cade (begin-run! merging main down) and reverted: attach! spawns the agent before it writes the release on main, so the branch read :held (seon.server-test/an-agent-reads-its-task-running, red by timing). The cause: the branch keeps a copy of a fact whose one writer is root, and the order of spawn and release is not the data's. The design: begin-run-tx writes no :seon.task/execution; my.task/current and the task part of @ctx read execution from main as heard (one pull join from main's value the agent already holds, heard-main), and attach! writes the release on main before it spawns, in one transaction with the spawn's record (no timing). Deletes: :seon.task/execution from begin-run-tx, and its copy on every branch. Estimate: one more read of main's value per task read (an entity lookup, ~10k ticks). Proofs, red first: seon.server-test/an-agent-reads-its-task-running (kept); seon.agent-test/a-branch-writes-no-task-execution (an agent's branch, after begin-run!, holds no :seon.task/execution datom of its own; red today); design guard seon.main-test/one-writer-per-task-execution (a law over src: :seon.task/execution is written only by seon.task's root-side writes)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-branch-writes-no-task-execution seon.main-test/one-writer-per-task-execution seon.server-test/an-agent-reads-its-task-running}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-stateful-process-is-a-flow-agent-in-one-tree-with-ports-named-by-message-keys"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner rulings: one unified design with duplicates hunted down; the Flow way; root is not special, it is the first agent; resources made at startup and released at shutdown; types are schemas; port names fully namespaced; and (2026-11-14) \"once the flow system is up and running ... we must only use flow based systems for stateful processes. If we still have holdouts we need to port them\" (runtime, the Flow specialist, ports them; plan item 3). Already landed: jolt.async.remote.flow/fork! closing inherited streams, test children as procs of their agent's graph, the agent rows (:seon.agent/parent, branch, commit, t and the four options), :seon.process/exit and end, the link's two unions, a request's address; :seon.step/request-id and :seon.step/answer in the schema. What is left, the holdouts [M, survey 2026-11-14 at 69b2b15f49+, lines re-read at HEAD 6184c92521]: (H1) timers outside Flow: root's :stall-check poll (system.clj:939-941, :1064-1067) -> task seon.agent-proc/a-stalled-eval-ends-with-its-error; the rest -> seon.step/every-wait-past-a-bound-is-a-flow-message-from-the-one-timer. (H2) seon.agent/state, one process-global atom shared by the agent's three procs (agent.clj:36; 52 swap!/reset! in agent.clj, 21 in repl.clj, 6 in turn.clj) -> task seon.agent/the-agents-state-is-declared. (H3) threads where a fiber does: seon.repl/serve-item! starts two a/io-thread per granted item though its docstring says a fiber (repl.clj:1357-1362), and the test-child exit watch one io-thread per child on poller/exit-chan, a channel already (agent.clj:1352): both go blocks (the exit watch with task a-refused-exit-watch-is-an-answer). (H4) seon.server's accept thread and one OS thread per connection, unbounded (server.clj:635-637): an edge reader per connection on a fiber (Jolt sockets park on the poller), connections bounded by a spec'd config entry. (H5) three request-id schemes for one pattern (:rid/:tag in seon.step/ask and its callers; fx's :dest {:agent :op :k}; the agent's :k from :tx-seq): one key, :seon.step/request-id, and one asker helper. (H6) process-global caches (server/held-index, doc/indexes, ctx's held and moved-since, vector/kept-rows, program's held forms, ratom's queues, system/tree-runs, system/uncaught): derived data, reactions under one derivation (plan item 5; not runtime's to build, runtime reviews that it runs as Flow procs). Kept, each with its reason (written at its site): edge reader threads for a blocking read (fx/read-lines!, the fork server's event reader, the agent's socket reader), seon.intr's interrupt stack (Chez's handler), the shutdown hook (an OS signal), :mixed workers for blocking foreign calls. The older remaining steps (clones and the fork server as a Flow graph with root's template child; one-ask; one-wait-on-main; one writer in root with senders resolving tempids; warm child at most 5 ms) stay as written in git at 1ff33e0b9c and are H5 and the writer's, each a slice. Runtime builds H1-H5, in the order: H1 (the stall slice), H3, H5, H2, H4. Deletes: per slice, the thread, timer or atom it ports (named in each landing). Estimate: per slice no added work per message; H3 and H4 remove one OS thread per item, child and connection. Proofs, red first, one per holdout: seon.agent-proc-test/a-request-past-its-bound-is-stalled-with-no-poll (H1); seon.agent-test/an-agent-running-three-granted-items-holds-no-thread-per-item (H3); seon.server-test/a-connection-holds-no-os-thread (H4); seon.step-test/every-ask-answers-by-one-request-id (H5); design guard seon.main-test/no-thread-is-started-outside-a-named-edge (a law over src: a/io-thread, Thread. and future only at the sites docs/failure-points.md names). Item (4), one-writer, measured and closed 2026-11-14 (flow; root's rule: build only if the wait grows with n): n = 1, 4, 16 processes each committing 10 datoms back to back to its own branch on one LMDB store: median 5.7, 6.6, 7.7 ms, throughput 128, 453, 1,584 commits/s (12.4x at 16), so LMDB's write lock holds ~0.1 ms of a 5.7 ms commit; per-branch writers stay, since one writer would serialize the 98% that runs in parallel. Still unmeasured: a merge commit's lock hold."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-proc-test/a-request-past-its-bound-is-stalled-with-no-poll seon.agent-test/an-agent-running-three-granted-items-holds-no-thread-per-item seon.main-test/no-thread-is-started-outside-a-named-edge seon.server-test/a-connection-holds-no-os-thread seon.step-test/every-ask-answers-by-one-request-id}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.step/every-seon-proc-follows-one-flow-standard-with-a-runnable-example-each"}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-of-main-applies-every-change-main-made"
  :seon.task/plan "Runtime's half of the one merge (seon.merge/merge-main, agreed 2026-11-14 by data-2, runtime, context-4 and deps; its (3)). The correctness half (every datom main wrote that the branch did not also change at the same entity and attribute is taken; carry-tx and carried-platform-attrs deleted) is data's: seon.merge/a-tasks-content-is-a-key-of-the-one-merge; the merged db as main's head plus the branch's own diff, one force-branch!, is data-2's in seon.merge/a-merge-of-main-costs-what-the-branch-changed (1)-(2). This task is the load half only. Measured [M, 2026-11-14 16:15, scratch spawn at context's branch, main 1,484 names ahead]: seon.agent/merge-main! 9.14B ticks and 51.9 GB, of which seon.program/load-changes! 2.81B (1,117 load-form!s, 2.46M ticks and ~29 MB each). What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: merge-main! loads main's changed forms into the running agent (program/load-changes!, agent.clj:587); the agent names the program it holds as a reference :program, set at its start (agent.clj:2212), read by seon.agent/program-db (agent.clj:262-267). The cause: the running process is brought to main's program by evaluating main's moves, O(main's change), though the warm fork server already holds main's program compiled. The design: (1) merge-main! loads none of main's forms (a process keeps its program until its next start, plan item 3): the branch moves, :program stays, so the agent is behind its branch; (2) its test children load-diff! from :program to the branch first (seon.program/load-diff!, the forms that differ only), so a verdict judges the branch; (3) at the run's next :waiting between turns (after the turn's record, before the next model call; context-4), the agent proc asks root to re-fork it from the warm fork server under its key, its walk value carried in the spawn's fork args (turn number and inbox are branch facts), and the resume path load-diff!s only the branch's own forms; a run past its cap or :done/:failed ends instead. Deletes: merge-main!'s load-changes! of main's moves (agent.clj:587) and its :load-errors answer. Estimate: one fork (~65 ms of fork(2), ~6.4 ms wait) + k x 2.46M ticks for the branch's k changed forms, against 2.81B for main's 1,117: at k = 2 about 5M plus the fork. Proofs, red first: seon.agent-test/a-merge-of-main-loads-none-of-mains-forms-into-the-agent (merge-main! of one changed form calls no load-form! and program-db is unchanged); seon.agent-test/a-test-child-of-an-agent-behind-its-branch-runs-the-branchs-program; seon.agent-test/a-merge-of-main-costs-the-branchs-change (a branch two forms ahead of a main that moved 100 forms: O(2) form loads, held to the arithmetic above); seon.turn-test/the-next-turns-request-bytes-are-equal-across-the-re-fork."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-merge-of-main-costs-the-branchs-change seon.agent-test/a-merge-of-main-loads-none-of-mains-forms-into-the-agent seon.agent-test/a-test-child-of-an-agent-behind-its-branch-runs-the-branchs-program seon.turn-test/the-next-turns-request-bytes-are-equal-across-the-re-fork}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-child-starts-on-its-branchs-environment"
  :seon.task/plan "Plan Stage 0.5; owner 2026-11-14: bootstrap checks once, runtime guarantees, no guards. Today a test child is a fork of root's agent and carries root's config, environment, heap, threads and libraries, patched piecewise by `seon.fx/checkout-env!` and ``seon.config/hold-checkout!`` (deleted since) (agent.clj:1254). Fix: hold-env! is every started process's first act (agent init, test child start, root boot); it brings program and config in place and refuses a pin, native library or binary mismatch as :seon.process/env-mismatch, reported as a platform problem, never a verdict; a child for another environment is spawned on that branch, never forked across. Reads resources/seon/environment.edn (deps). Deletes checkout-env! and hold-checkout!. Owners: tests builds, runtime reviews (root, 2026-11-14). What exists [M, tests 16:45, measured by seon.agent-test/an-agent-starts-on-the-config-its-branch-states]: an agent spawned on a branch holds its branch's program and config at its start (init!'s load-diff!, seon.program/hold-config!), and a config a branch cannot hold is refused at its write (seon.program/config-refused), so its test children fork from a process already on the branch's config; only the environment variables of a session's checkout are patched in the child (seon.fx/checkout-env!). Slices: (A) DONE before this plan (the config is held at the agent's start and checked at its write: no second check at the start); (B) the binary and pin mismatch refused at the same start from deps' one binary identity (plan item 3a), replacing seon.system/tree-refusal; (C) checkout-env! deleted with the session edit path that needs no checkout (root: the bin/land routing is cancelled; sessions work on branches in the store)."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-child-of-an-agent-on-another-pin-is-spawned-on-its-runtime seon.agent-test/a-child-whose-config-differs-from-its-branch-is-refused-and-stores-no-verdict seon.agent-test/a-test-child-of-a-branch-reads-its-branchs-config seon.main-test/no-program-loads-outside-the-one-start}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-writes-are-one-commit"
  :seon.task/plan "An agent's writes are grouped into commits already: transact! applies each write ahead of the commit (seon.writer's dw/apply!), and commit! (dw/commit-pending!) writes what is pending once, when the REPL goes idle (seon.repl/agent-step) or at a turn's eval. A commit costs ~440k ticks of persist and 34 nodes whatever its size (data-2's split of seon.db/a-commit-persists-at-the-cost-of-the-nodes-it-writes [M]), so k writes in one commit cost one persist. Some call sites commit in the middle of an eval, right after their own write, and each such commit is a persist the eval's own end would have saved. Owner: flow (root's go, 2026-11-14 21:37). Each site, with its reason, read 2026-11-14 at jolt/flow 78fdc0b02d. KEPT, a reader needs the write now: seon.agent/merge-main! (agent.clj ~585): the merge reads the committed head (@conn); seon.agent/clear-end! (~724): root's pull-end (seon.reconcile) reads the branch's end, and a wake that dies before another commit must not leave the last wake's end there; seon.agent/write-end! (~743): root's judge reads the end, and the process may exit next with no idle commit to come; seon.agent/prove! (~1047): the test service runs at the committed head's digest and the test child forks from it; seon.agent/judge!'s first commit (~1858): the gate reads the committed head; seon.agent/clone! (~2115): root branches this branch at its committed head; seon.turn's and seon.repl/run-calls' and insert!'s per-eval commits: a turn holds the :repl step for all its evals, so no idle commit comes until its end, and root's render shows each eval as it lands (seon.turn, the docstring at turn.clj ~215). GO, nobody outside this process reads the write before the eval ends, whose idle commit does each one's job: my.agent/cite! and uncite! (my/agent.clj ~73, ~86: the page draws it at the eval's end, ms later); my.branch's import (my/branch.clj ~40); judge!'s gate-verdict commit (~1876: the status reads the verdicts in this process). TO DECIDE BY READING ITS READER FIRST: seon.agent/deliver! (~466): a turn delivers at its start and then waits on the model, so its next commit can be minutes away; it stays unless root's readers of delivered messages (what marks a message delivered on main, and whether root delivers it again meanwhile) are shown not to need it; say who reads it. The first proof measures the burst before anything is deleted: seon.agent_proc keeps :txs, the transactions each :committed carried (agent.clj's commit! sends it; agent_proc.clj ~259 drops it today), as a count by commit in its state, read by Flow's ping, so the commits per eval and the writes per commit of real agents are read, not guessed. Estimate: per eval, (commits - 1) x ~440k ticks saved, and 34 nodes a commit not written (~26 KB a node [H]); keeping :txs is one map update per commit, ~1k ticks and ~100 B. Deletes: the commit! calls listed under GO; the idle commit at the eval's end does each one's job. No functionality removed: a reader that needs a write now keeps its commit, and says who it is. Proofs, red first: seon.agent-test/an-evals-writes-are-one-commit (an eval that cites three addresses makes one commit carrying 3 transactions; red today: four commits); seon.agent-test/a-commits-transactions-are-counted (a :committed frame's :txs is kept by the agent's proc and read in its ping; red today: dropped)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-commits-transactions-are-counted seon.agent-test/an-evals-writes-are-one-commit}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-failure-marked-through-the-server-overruns-intermittently"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by deps 2026-11-15 [M]: seon.agent-test/a-failure-marked-through-the-server-holds-the-task overran its 300 s bound in four runs (context's land 00:34, runtime's 01:39, tests' 01:43, deps' reached-set run 08:50, each its shard-N-overran evidence), green elsewhere at ~252k ticks, and green rerun alone at 08:5x. Evidence kept: side-session-notes/local_8f79e2bb-ff27-4bdc-ba2c-e0f2f4a0d867/evidence/a-failure-marked-overran-2026-11-15-0850 (dumps, lsof, logs). In it, process 28406's fiber 0 is the fork server's serve loop (seon.fork-server/main, server-step, seon.repl/serve!) parked on a channel wait; its 1,890M ticks are that fiber's whole lifetime, inherited through the fork, not the test's work (corrected by deps 2026-11-15: an earlier reading called it busy). So the overrun is a wait, not work [read]: find what each process in the evidence waits on, and who should have answered it. Each overrun costs its land a full re-prove. Cause to find at both ends before any fix; no retry, no raised bound (AGENTS.md §3, §9)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-failure-marked-through-the-server-holds-the-task}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.agent-test{a-failure-marked-through-the-server-holds-the-task {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 252000, :bytes 20000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-namespace-that-does-not-load-fails-only-its-own-tests"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (the one loader). Measured at root [tests-4, 2026-11-15]: one form that did not load (seon.reads-test, Jolt's emit-quoted on a #uuid) failed a test agent's whole program load, so every test in every batch answered :seon.prove/no-verdict (482 load failures, 30 no-verdicts a test) and main's run proved nothing; the failure reached main only as error facts no status or page shows. The existing mechanism: the agent's program load (seon.agent-proc's load of the branch's program). Fix: a namespace that does not load answers its load error to its own tests (each a red naming the form), and every other namespace loads and runs; the run states the namespaces it could not load. Cost: none on a good load; O(namespaces) on a bad one. Proof, red first: an agent whose branch holds one namespace that does not load runs the other namespaces' tests green and answers that namespace's tests with its load error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-namespace-that-does-not-load-fails-only-its-own-tests}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.agent{load-program {:n 300, :order :linear, :ticks 100000, :bytes 100000}}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-run-that-just-merged-main-does-not-merge-it-again"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by context (2026-11-15, landing seon.turn's no-wait steps, flow agreed). [M] seon.turn/start! merges main (my.branch/merge-main!), and the run's first turn-begin then finds (seon.agent/main-moved? (:base @agent/state) (agent/heard-main)) true again, so it asks a second {:merge} that brings nothing: ~2.3M ticks / ~12 MB a run (seon.turn-test/a-turn-sends-its-model-call-and-returns' profile: my.branch/merge-main! 2.3M, seon.agent/merge-main! 1.8M). Cause to find, both ends read first: start!'s merge leaves :base or the heard main behind what main-moved? compares (H: heard-main is read before the merge's commit, or :base is not the merged commit). Fix at the cause: after a merge the agent's base is the main it merged, so main-moved? is false until main moves. Estimate: the second merge removed, 0 ticks added; main-moved? stays one commit-id compare."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/estimate {seon.agent/main-moved? {:n 1 :order :constant :ticks 1000 :bytes 1000}}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/a-runs-first-turn-after-its-start-merge-asks-no-merge}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-process-tests-verdict-does-not-depend-on-its-batch"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by data, 2026-11-15 [jolt/data 8bbbc8d441, verdicts e254b0cebcb1075a, 0ffad1e0b600dfae]: seon.agent-test/a-process-test-gets-its-verdict-while-the-agent-can-act and seon.agent-test/a-process-test-green-at-its-digest-asks-no-gate are red in a 264-test run of agent/contract/schema/procs (~19:16) and green alone (19:3x), same tree: \"nothing left unjudged\" failed, [:deferred] still held the test itself (the line did not start \"green: 1 of 1\"). Root's class: a verdict that depends on which process (here, which batch) runs it; E's acceptance. PRIOR WORK to read before a cause is named: both ends of the gate's answer for a deferred process test (seon.agent/ask-gate and send-gate!, the gate's verdict at its digest, judge!'s :deferred), and what a big batch shares that a lone run does not (the results branch's verdicts at the test's digest written by an earlier test of the batch, the file cache, the gate's queue). Fix at the cause; proof: each green inside a batch that holds the other tests first. Measured 2026-11-15 22:2x (tests): the item left :deferred is the ENCLOSING deftest (seon.agent-test/a-process-test-..., a process test), not the agent's proof (data's target/prove/20261115T191356-6660 shard-0.log:132, shard-3.log:137; the line says \"outstanding: 1 process tests with no verdict: the tests your change reaches\"): so on the agent's branch the outer test's digest moved from base's, though it names the agent's var only as a quoted symbol (data, seon.program :quoted). In shard-3 it ran second, after a-checkouts-plan-names-its-process-tests-steps-and-build on the same shared root (both write a seon.repl-test process test). Green: the pair alone (jolt/tests, 22:2x); bin/test --all split the ordered pair into two shards, so the order case is unmeasured. Next observation, at the REPL: in the agent, (:selected (seon.agent/test-plan (seon.agent/db) (:base @seon.agent/state) #{})) after a prior agent's test, and which input of the outer test's digest differs from base's."
